   "notes": "Primary-source facts (WebFetch): design 1 = equal-status agents + shared coordination file + locks: locks held too long / never released, agents failed while holding locks, \"twenty agents would slow down to the effective throughput of two or three\". Design 2 = optimistic concurrency: more robust, but \"no agent took responsibility for hard problems or end-to-end implementation\". Design 3 = planners (can spawn sub-planners) + workers that do not coordinate with each other + judge per cycle with fresh restarts. An integrator role was removed (bottleneck; workers resolved conflicts themselves). Models assigned by role (GPT-5.2 as planner; Opus 4.5 \"stops earlier and takes shortcuts\"), hence heterogeneous=yes. Lessons: \"Too little structure and agents conflict, duplicate work, and drift. Too much structure creates fragility\"; periodic fresh starts still needed against drift. Strongest practitioner evidence AGAINST pure role-free self-organization at scale. No controls/statistics. fastrender repo HEAD 200 (the browser code per post).",
